# Copy to apps/web/.env.local for local dev. Defaults below match docker-compose.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/travel
# Any non-empty string locally; `npx auth secret` for real deployments
AUTH_SECRET=dev-secret-change-me
# Enables the username-only Dev Login provider (any username, no password,
# yields `dev-<name>` — and post-M11 that identity inherits whatever trip
# memberships it already has). Required but not sufficient: the gate in
# apps/web/src/lib/devLogin.ts also requires VERCEL_ENV to be anything other
# than "production". VERCEL_ENV is set by Vercel and never by us, so a
# production deployment cannot register the provider however this var was
# scoped — this line is no longer the only thing standing between production
# and credential-less sign-in.
AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=true
# The inbox dev-login identities are plus-addressed off: signing in as `alice`
# yields alice / dev-alice / <local>+alice@<domain>, so invite mail can
# actually be received while every dev user stays distinct. Defaults to
# dev@example.com, which is well-formed but undeliverable by design — set this
# to your own address if you want to actually read the mail the app sends.
# AUTH_DEV_EMAIL_BASE=you@example.com
# Google OAuth (optional locally, required in prod) — console.cloud.google.com
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# The reusable super code for the invite gate (M11a link 3) — one shared string
# that admits anyone who has never signed in before. Optional and OFF by
# default: unset or blank admits NOBODY (`server/admission.ts` refuses before
# comparing), so a deployment that forgets this variable is closed, not open.
# Comparison is constant-time, so mint it from a CSPRNG rather than choosing
# something memorable — e.g. `openssl rand -base64 24`.
# ROTATION NEEDS A REDEPLOY. Vercel injects environment variables at build
# time, so changing the value in the dashboard does nothing until the next
# deployment. Prefer single-use `invite_codes` rows for most people and keep
# this for active onboarding; do not treat it as revocable in an emergency.
# UNCOMMENTED ON PURPOSE, and this value is not a secret. `pnpm setup` copies
# this file to apps/web/.env.local, and `db:reset` truncates `users` (it derives
# its table list from the schema), so the first sign-in after a reseed is always
# a brand-new account — with no code set, M11a's gate refuses it and
# `pnpm db:reseed` fails on a fresh clone. Same class of local-only default as
# AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=true above.
#
# NEVER deploy this value. Preview and Production set their own in the Vercel
# dashboard (`openssl rand -base64 24`), and env vars there come from the
# dashboard, never from this file. An unset variable admits nobody, which is
# the safe default this line deliberately trades away FOR LOCAL DEV ONLY.
INVITE_SUPER_CODE=local-dev-only-not-a-secret
# Geocoding key (ADR-007). Required wherever activity-location geocoding runs
# (preview/prod); locally /api/geocode errors without it. Free key at
# locationiq.com; set in Vercel env for preview + production.
LOCATIONIQ_API_KEY=
# Vercel AI Gateway key (ADR-015). Required wherever schema-derived AI
# generation runs (preview/prod); locally aiModel() throws without it.
# vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway; set in Vercel env for preview + production.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# Gateway model id. Defaults to a cheap model per the closed-action-space
# thesis (ADR-015); override to try a different model.
# AI_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5
# Model for the /ask pre-turn intent classifier ONLY (askIntent.ts) — one
# binary verdict, no tools, ~200 input tokens, on every editor turn. Defaults
# to AI_MODEL, so setting AI_MODEL alone still moves both and nothing changes
# until this is set deliberately. Set it to point the cheapest/fastest model
# you have at the classification while a larger one answers; the turn's own
# `classification.model`, `.latencyMs` and `.usage` in the ai.ask log are what
# to judge a candidate on. The verdict is a structured field, not a parsed
# word, so a reasoning model is a valid choice here (KI-88).
# AI_CLASSIFIER_MODEL=
# Spend ceilings (project review H1/L4). All optional — every one has a safe
# default in `server/quota.ts`, and a malformed value falls back to that
# default rather than to unlimited. Raise them only with the gateway bill in
# view: one request makes several model round-trips, and the step ceilings
# below are what meter that (KI-67) — a `/ask` turn takes up to 8.
# The global ceilings are per deployment, and exist because the per-user one
# only bounds a single account — sign-up-and-repeat is what they answer.
# AI_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER_HOURLY=30
# AI_RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_HOURLY=300
# AI_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER_DAILY=100
# AI_RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_DAILY=1000
# Geocode ceilings are daily because LocationIQ's free tier is a daily 5,000,
# so the global one sits under the real vendor quota rather than just
# smoothing bursts.
# GEOCODE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER_DAILY=300
# GEOCODE_RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_DAILY=4000
# Flags Explorer auth (Vercel Flags). Required in preview/production for the
# .well-known/vercel/flags discovery endpoint; unset locally is fine — the
# endpoint just 401s. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
FLAGS_SECRET=
# LOCAL/CI ESCAPE HATCH ONLY. NEVER set this in a Vercel environment — it
# overrides the `ai-live` feature flag entirely, which is the one control
# standing between a shared deployment and an unbounded AI token bill.
# false = the AI endpoint returns a simulated plan and calls no model.
AI_LIVE=false
# PREVIEW-ONLY debug gate for POST /api/dev/reset-demo-data (see
# apps/web/src/lib/demoDataReset.ts). With this set to "true" AND Vercel's
# own VERCEL_ENV reading "preview" (VERCEL_ENV is set by Vercel, never by
# us, so production can never satisfy this regardless of this var), the
# route wipes the signed-in caller's own trips and reseeds the 14-day Japan
# demo trip. Unset or any other value: the route 404s as if it didn't exist.
# SEED_DEMO_DATA=true
# ─── Sentry (errors, tracing, profiles, AI agent monitoring, metrics) ───
# All of these are OPTIONAL. Unset, the app reports to the project DSN baked
# into apps/web/sentry.shared.ts at the sample rates documented there — which
# is what every environment does today. See ADR-032 and
# docs/guidelines/observability-and-telemetry.md.
#
# Where events go. Public by design (a DSN is a write-only ingest key and ships
# in the client bundle), so this is NOT a secret. Set it to the EMPTY STRING to
# turn Sentry off entirely — locally that is often what you want, since a dev
# session otherwise files its own noise against the shared project.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
#
# Source-map upload at build time. THIS ONE IS A SECRET — it can write to the
# Sentry project. Read only by `withSentryConfig` in apps/web/next.config.ts,
# never by app code, and never in a bundle. Unset locally is fine (builds skip
# the upload); set it in Vercel so production stack traces resolve to source.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
#
# Sample rates, all 0..1. A value that is not a number in that range is
# IGNORED in favour of the default rather than becoming NaN — see
# `sampleRate` in apps/web/sentry.shared.ts for why that matters.
#
# The two rates below come in PAIRS, and the pairing is load-bearing rather
# than tidy: `apps/web/sentry.shared.ts` is imported by the browser bundle, and
# Next.js inlines only NEXT_PUBLIC_* names there. Setting the server name alone
# turns the rate down on the server and leaves the BROWSER at its default, with
# nothing anywhere saying so. Set both, or set the public one (it wins in both
# runtimes). See ADR-032.
#
# Traces (default 1). Turn this down and you turn the AI agent traces down
# with it — the gen_ai spans ride on the same sampling decision.
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=1
# CPU profiles, browser and server (default 1). Profiling only runs while a
# sampled root span is open, so this is cheaper than it looks.
# SENTRY_PROFILE_SESSION_SAMPLE_RATE=1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_PROFILE_SESSION_SAMPLE_RATE=1
# Session Replay (defaults 0.1 and 1.0). Public — they are read in the browser.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_REPLAYS_SESSION_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_REPLAYS_ON_ERROR_SAMPLE_RATE=1.0

# Local port overrides — WEB_PORT default lives in apps/web/src/config.ts
# (M0 retro: 3000/5432 are taken on this machine). POSTGRES_PORT only affects
# docker-compose's published port; update DATABASE_URL above to match.
# WEB_PORT=3001
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# Overrides the whole base URL (scheme + host + port) rather than just the port.
# Deliberately NOT named BASE_URL: that name belongs to Vite, which assigns its
# own `BASE_URL` ("/", the public base path) into process.env inside every
# Vitest worker — so a config reading `BASE_URL` reads "/" in tests. See KI-72
# and the comment in apps/web/src/config.ts.
# WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Remote database URLs are never stored in this repo. Preview/Production values
# live in Vercel env scopes; the production migration secret lives in GitHub
# Actions (PRODUCTION_DATABASE_URL). See docs/guidelines/environments-and-deploys.md.
